WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.997
Hello and welcome. I'm James Murphy.

00:00:01.997 --> 00:00:07.693
In this video, we're going to learn how to programmatically traverse your&nbsp;Python code from within Python itself.

00:00:07.693 --> 00:00:11.395
Then we'll use that ability to help us write&nbsp;custom checks for our code

00:00:11.395 --> 00:00:15.522
to prevent us from making common errors and keep our code quality&nbsp;high.

00:00:15.522 --> 00:00:18.626
Basically, the goal is to write a custom plugin for flake8.

00:00:18.626 --> 00:00:20.407
flake8 is what's called&nbsp;a linter.

00:00:20.407 --> 00:00:23.434
It runs on your source code and then tells you if there's anything wrong.

00:00:23.434 --> 00:00:26.560
In this case,&nbsp;it didn't find anything wrong with the cool_module.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:31.765
However, if we add an unused variable into our&nbsp;function and then run the linter again, it'll complain.

00:00:31.765 --> 00:00:34.743
Local variable x is assigned but never&nbsp;used.

00:00:34.743 --> 00:00:38.031
Linters can also warn you about stylistic or other code style things.

00:00:38.031 --> 00:00:42.521
Like, in this case, it's&nbsp;telling me that there's no newline at the end of the file.

00:00:42.521 --> 00:00:46.326
A linter like flake8 already has a ton&nbsp;of really good rules built into it

00:00:46.326 --> 00:00:49.140
and for those that you don't like you can just turn them off.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:52.160
But&nbsp;what can you do if you want to add your own rule?

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:57.360
Let's say, just for example purposes that, you&nbsp;want to disallow local imports in functions.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:00.720
There's nothing necessarily wrong with&nbsp;having an import inside a function.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:06.160
But sometimes, these function level imports are&nbsp;used as a crutch to avoid cyclic dependency issues.

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:12.251
Basically, a local import might be hiding a deeper&nbsp;architectural issue and we just want to prevent that.

00:01:12.251 --> 00:01:14.757
But then, we run into a kind of big issue.

00:01:14.757 --> 00:01:20.256
If I asked you to find all the local imports in a module, how would you do it?

00:01:20.256 --> 00:01:26.242
You may reach&nbsp;for regular expressions and try to match sort of the form of what a local import looks like.

00:01:26.242 --> 00:01:29.386
But&nbsp;even for this simple task of finding local imports,

00:01:29.386 --> 00:01:32.915
trying to do it with a regular expression is going&nbsp;to be an absolute nightmare.

00:01:32.915 --> 00:01:36.012
The issue with trying to use a regular expression is that

00:01:36.012 --> 00:01:39.653
text is not&nbsp;the natural way that we think about Python code.

00:01:39.653 --> 00:01:43.644
I don't think about these concepts in terms&nbsp;of the literal characters that make them up.

00:01:43.644 --> 00:01:49.200
Instead, I think on a more abstract level&nbsp;in terms of classes, functions, for-loops etc.

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:54.560
Well, the good news is that Python itself also&nbsp;thinks this way and it can expose this data to you.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.560
We can see this representation&nbsp;of the data using the ast module.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:03.808
When I run ast on my cool_module, this is what it&nbsp;looks like.

00:02:03.808 --> 00:02:05.317
It shows me I have a module

00:02:05.317 --> 00:02:09.183
whose body starts with a function definition of a function&nbsp;named cool

00:02:09.183 --> 00:02:11.969
with an argument named n and so on.

00:02:13.424 --> 00:02:15.860
This is called an abstract syntax&nbsp;tree.

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:20.534
And it's exactly the kind of data structure that we need in order&nbsp;to analyze our Python source code.

00:02:20.534 --> 00:02:25.440
Running the ast module on a source file&nbsp;will print out the abstract syntax tree.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:31.120
But we can also import ast from within our&nbsp;own code and access that tree at runtime.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:33.892
In this example, we read the code from the source&nbsp;module.

00:02:33.892 --> 00:02:37.022
Then we pass it to the ast.parse() function

00:02:37.022 --> 00:02:40.650
This gives us back an in-memory representation of&nbsp;the tree.

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:44.833
Printing out the node that we get, we see the top level module object.

00:02:44.833 --> 00:02:47.763
All these ast objects&nbsp;have an underscore fields attribute.

00:02:47.763 --> 00:02:50.680
So, you can see what things are inside of them.

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:53.840
You can also&nbsp;find this in the docs for the ast module.

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:56.913
So, we see a module has a body field.

00:02:56.913 --> 00:03:02.662
Printing&nbsp;that out, we see the body is made of a list of statements, in this case, just one function&nbsp;definition.

00:03:02.662 --> 00:03:08.320
We can dig further and further and get things like the name, arguments&nbsp;or body of the function definition.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:13.680
Everything that you see in this printout of&nbsp;the ast, we can actually now access from runtime.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:18.701
So, I start with the top level node object&nbsp;and then I could say node.body get the zeroth element

00:03:18.701 --> 00:03:23.186
that says function definition and then&nbsp;dot name if I wanted to see what the name was.

00:03:23.186 --> 00:03:24.210
So, this is great.

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:27.867
This tree like structure&nbsp;is now what allows us to traverse our Python code

00:03:27.867 --> 00:03:31.959
in terms of Python constructs&nbsp;rather than literal characters.

00:03:31.959 --> 00:03:36.960
ast also provides this NodeVisitor&nbsp;class that we're meant to derive from.

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:42.640
A node visitor makes it really easy for us to walk&nbsp;through the entire tree and visit all of the nodes.

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:45.284
So here, we call visit on the root node

00:03:45.284 --> 00:03:49.061
and what&nbsp;visit is going to do is, we'll just print out the node

00:03:49.061 --> 00:03:53.486
and then we call this generic_visit which&nbsp;will visit all of the children of this node. 

00:03:53.486 --> 00:03:54.172
Here's the output.

00:03:54.172 --> 00:03:57.223
You can see it starts with the&nbsp;module then goes into the function definition.

00:03:57.223 --> 00:04:02.098
And basically, we get a single call for every single&nbsp;node in the tree.

00:04:02.098 --> 00:04:05.245
But normally, when you're linting your code and you have a specific rule in mind,

00:04:05.245 --> 00:04:07.500
it doesn't just apply to every kind of object.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:11.086
It doesn't apply to modules and classes and&nbsp;functions.

00:04:11.086 --> 00:04:14.019
Maybe, I have a rule about a common error involving for-loops.

00:04:14.019 --> 00:04:18.240
In that case, instead&nbsp;of visiting everything, I can just say visit_For.

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:23.481
Now, when I run the code, I only get two calls&nbsp;because there were only two for-loops in the cool_module.

00:04:23.481 --> 00:04:26.364
You can look it up in the ast docs for what&nbsp;all these names are.

00:04:26.364 --> 00:04:30.564
But basically, there's one for every single possible Python construct.

00:04:30.564 --> 00:04:35.396
Okay, back&nbsp;to the goal. I want to warn if there's any import inside of a function.

00:04:35.396 --> 00:04:38.236
I'm confident that if I&nbsp;could get flake8 to call my code,

00:04:38.236 --> 00:04:40.499
then I would be able to find all the local imports.

00:04:40.499 --> 00:04:43.029
So, how do I get&nbsp;flake8 to call my code?

00:04:43.029 --> 00:04:45.376
It's actually surprisingly simple.

00:04:45.376 --> 00:04:49.001
I'll make a class for my plugin and then&nbsp;give it a name and version.

00:04:49.001 --> 00:04:52.754
This name and version are required and they're going to show up in the&nbsp;help text.

00:04:52.754 --> 00:04:56.054
Then make an init function that takes an argument named tree.

00:04:56.054 --> 00:05:00.160
flake8 will inspect&nbsp;the signature and know to pass the ast here.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:02.973
flake8 will construct my plugin and then call run.

00:05:02.973 --> 00:05:06.709
And run is supposed to yield any errors that it finds.

00:05:06.709 --> 00:05:10.010
Here, I just want to see it run. So, I'm just&nbsp;yielding nothing.

00:05:10.010 --> 00:05:15.745
Then we create this dot flake8 configuration file in order to tell flake8 where&nbsp;our plugin is.

00:05:15.745 --> 00:05:18.480
It uses a standard ini file format.

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:23.440
Create this block for local-plugins and&nbsp;then we're going to be creating an extension.

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:27.840
You could tell it about multiple extensions&nbsp;like this. But for now, let's just stick to one.

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:31.885
On the left side, you tell it what code prefix all&nbsp;of your errors are going to use.

00:05:31.885 --> 00:05:34.050
In this case, I'm using, like, mcoding 1.

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:37.528
So, I'm saying that all&nbsp;of my errors are going to start with MCOD1,

00:05:37.528 --> 00:05:40.817
so like, MCOD100, MCOD101 and so on.

00:05:40.817 --> 00:05:46.340
Then on&nbsp;the right hand side, use the dotted path to the module. In this case, the module is just in the same&nbsp;directory.

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:49.723
And then, a colon followed by the name of the class.

00:05:49.723 --> 00:05:53.533
Since my module is not in an installed&nbsp;package in the current virtual environment,

00:05:53.533 --> 00:05:57.172
I just need to tell flake8 using this paths equals&nbsp;current directory

00:05:57.172 --> 00:05:59.964
to look in the current directory for plugins.

00:05:59.964 --> 00:06:03.760
Otherwise, it's going to complain&nbsp;that it can't find the flake8_mcoding module

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:06.518
And if we go ahead and run flake8 on our cool_module,

00:06:06.518 --> 00:06:10.009
we see the running text that we printed out from the plugin.

00:06:10.009 --> 00:06:12.775
Okay, our plugin is running but it&nbsp;currently doesn't do anything.

00:06:12.775 --> 00:06:16.127
It just yields no errors. So, how do we yield an error?

00:06:16.127 --> 00:06:19.206
flake8 expects you to yield errors as 4 tuples

00:06:19.206 --> 00:06:23.567
including the line_number, the column offset,&nbsp;the message of the error and the class.

00:06:23.567 --> 00:06:27.797
The line number_column and message are pretty&nbsp;self-explanatory. So, what's this class thing?

00:06:27.797 --> 00:06:32.000
Well, it's actually not used for anything&nbsp;at all. It's just completely ignored.

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:36.800
But it does have to be there and I generally&nbsp;just return the class that generated the error.

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:38.446
Just to see if everything's working,

00:06:38.446 --> 00:06:43.692
we'll just&nbsp;try always yielding an error, say, at line one at offset zero.

00:06:43.692 --> 00:06:47.200
Running flake8 on the&nbsp;cool_module, we now see our custom error.

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:53.543
Note that, your error messages must start with the&nbsp;prefix that you specified in the configuration file, or they'll be ignored.

00:06:53.543 --> 00:06:56.586
The prefix I specified&nbsp;was MCOD1.

00:06:56.586 --> 00:07:01.240
If I change that to a MCOD2 and run it again, then I no longer see the error.

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:04.073
Let's change&nbsp;it back. But that's just something to be aware of.

00:07:04.073 --> 00:07:09.723
Getting back to the goal of finding imports inside&nbsp;of functions, let's make a NodeVisitor and try to find them.

00:07:09.723 --> 00:07:16.290
My NodeVisitor initializes an empty&nbsp;list of errors and will populate that whenever it finds an import inside of a function.

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:19.858
We'll use&nbsp;visit_FunctionDef to find all the function nodes.

00:07:19.858 --> 00:07:24.569
Starting from a function definition node, finding a&nbsp;local import is pretty easy.

00:07:24.569 --> 00:07:27.846
We just loop over all the children in the body of the function.

00:07:27.846 --> 00:07:34.869
Then if&nbsp;any child node is an instance of either an Import node or an ImportFrom node, then we found a local&nbsp;import.

00:07:34.869 --> 00:07:37.120
Then, we just create and append the error.

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:42.060
Note that, every node has a line number and offset.&nbsp;So, we can just forward those on to our error.

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:47.041
Here we're using the line number and offset of either&nbsp;the Import or ImportFrom statement.

00:07:47.041 --> 00:07:51.183
After visiting all the function definitions, all the errors will&nbsp;be stored in this list.

00:07:51.183 --> 00:07:55.000
So, now all we need to do is call our visitor from our plugin.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:57.004
Our plugin will&nbsp;create the visitor.

00:07:57.004 --> 00:08:00.806
Then we'll start the visiting process and pass in the root of the ast.

00:08:00.806 --> 00:08:04.000
Finally, we&nbsp;yield all the errors that the visitor had stored.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:07.144
Running flake8 on the cool_module now correctly&nbsp;shows us the error

00:08:07.144 --> 00:08:11.941
that we aren't allowed to use a local import and it even gets the line and call&nbsp;number correct.

00:08:11.941 --> 00:08:14.228
Okay, this works. We accomplished the goal.

00:08:14.228 --> 00:08:16.603
But we wouldn't want to create many different&nbsp;visitors

00:08:16.603 --> 00:08:19.990
if we had many different rules that we wanted to add to our linter.

00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:24.279
Traversing the entire&nbsp;ast is extremely expensive if you have a large codebase.

00:08:24.279 --> 00:08:28.172
I would actually refactor this a little&nbsp;bit in order to make it easier to add multiple checks

00:08:28.172 --> 00:08:30.741
without traversing the tree multiple times.

00:08:30.741 --> 00:08:34.246
So, what I did here was, I extracted basically the body of the check

00:08:34.246 --> 00:08:37.923
that was actually doing the&nbsp;checking to see if anything was wrong with the tree

00:08:37.923 --> 00:08:40.839
and I extracted that into a check function.

00:08:40.839 --> 00:08:44.842
I wrap it up in a class and put the message as a class attribute.

00:08:44.842 --> 00:08:50.897
So, for every kind of new check&nbsp;that I want to write, I just make a new class, I give it a message and a check function.

00:08:50.897 --> 00:08:55.168
I have the&nbsp;check function pass the list of errors that it's supposed to append to.

00:08:55.168 --> 00:09:00.097
Then in our node visitor, I&nbsp;just call the check function of every check class that I want to run.

00:09:00.097 --> 00:09:05.437
If you want to add options to&nbsp;your plugin like adding a strict flag or something like that, you can also do that.

00:09:05.437 --> 00:09:10.686
In your plugin,&nbsp;just create a @staticmethod add_options that takes an option_manager.

00:09:10.686 --> 00:09:12.917
You can import the option&nbsp;manager like this.

00:09:12.917 --> 00:09:16.466
If you've ever used argparse, then option manager will be very familiar to you.

00:09:16.466 --> 00:09:18.922
It's basically a thin wrapper around argparse.

00:09:18.922 --> 00:09:23.177
So, if I wanted to add some fancy variable&nbsp;as an option, then I would do it like this.

00:09:23.177 --> 00:09:26.811
Specify the flag that you want. What's the type&nbsp;of the variable?

00:09:26.811 --> 00:09:30.000
What's the name of the variable? This is going to show up in the help text.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:31.720
Give&nbsp;it a default value.

00:09:31.720 --> 00:09:36.968
This allows people to set it from their configuration file and specify any&nbsp;help text.

00:09:36.968 --> 00:09:42.345
At this point, if you run flake8 with --help, you'll see the documentation for your plugin.

00:09:42.345 --> 00:09:45.934
We see the --some-fancy-variable, the name n and our description.

00:09:45.934 --> 00:09:50.871
In order to get the values of any&nbsp;specified options, you need to add this parse_options function.

00:09:50.871 --> 00:09:55.970
It's another @staticmethod and&nbsp;it takes an options parameter which is an actual argparse.Namespace.

00:09:55.970 --> 00:09:59.664
Any variables you set will&nbsp;now just be attributes of this options variable.

00:09:59.664 --> 00:10:05.272
So, if you wanted to say enable strict mode or&nbsp;something based off of an option, you just set that as a flag here.

00:10:05.272 --> 00:10:08.726
And this gets called before&nbsp;the run method gets called.

00:10:08.726 --> 00:10:13.571
So, all of your options and whatever you set will be available before the&nbsp;run happens.

00:10:13.571 --> 00:10:16.132
If you want to test your knowledge, here's some homework.

00:10:16.132 --> 00:10:20.273
First problem: Write a plugin&nbsp;to warn against any use of eval.

00:10:20.273 --> 00:10:23.991
Don't worry about sneaky cases. Just check for literal eval.

00:10:23.991 --> 00:10:27.958
Next&nbsp;problem: Forbid the creation of any new metaclasses.

00:10:27.958 --> 00:10:34.046
And lastly: Write a plugin that for multiple&nbsp;assignments forbids multiple uses of the same name.

00:10:34.046 --> 00:10:37.983
This is a popular confusing example to throw&nbsp;around. Let's forbid it.

00:10:37.983 --> 00:10:41.937
The name a is used multiple times here and we don't want to allow that.

00:10:41.937 --> 00:10:45.494
See my&nbsp;video if you'd like an explanation of how multiple assignment works.

00:10:45.494 --> 00:10:49.062
And finally, if you're really&nbsp;serious about writing your own linting rules,

00:10:49.062 --> 00:10:51.805
you should really package your linting code.

00:10:51.805 --> 00:10:57.051
Right&nbsp;now, all of this code is just depending on all these files being in the same directory.

00:10:57.051 --> 00:10:58.613
That's&nbsp;very flaky.

00:10:58.613 --> 00:11:04.128
For a real project, I would recommend packaging your custom rules, so that you can&nbsp;just pip install them.

00:11:04.128 --> 00:11:08.977
See my video on automated testing if you'd like to get up to speed on&nbsp;how to package your projects.

00:11:08.977 --> 00:11:13.003
As always, thank you for watching. And thank you to my patrons&nbsp;
and donors for supporting me.

00:11:13.003 --> 00:11:14.310
See you next time.

